      "evidenceText": "Dianna Russini Set to Return to Media: What We Know Monday, November 16, 2026 More Sign In Sign Up Sports Football Basketball Baseball Hockey Soccer Women’s Sports College Sports More Sports Golf Tennis Motorsports Combat Sports Industries Media Finance Stadiums & Real Estate Sports Betting Law Retail Franchises Asset Class Tuned In Explore About Company News Studios Submit a Tip Advertise Contact Careers Shop First at FOS Media Dianna Russini Set to Return to Media: What We Know FOS has learned new details about Dianna Russini’s upcoming sports-media comeback. By Ryan Glasspiegel , Mark Maske , and Michael McCarthy Nov 06, 2026 | 10:48 am UPDATED Nov 06, 2026 | 07:23 pm Kirby Lee-Imagn Images Want more from Front Office Sports in your search results? Add Front Office Sports on Google Dianna Russini is poised to make a sports-media comeback soon, Front Office Sports has confirmed, several months after resigning from The Athletic in the aftermath of Page Six publishing photos showing her with Patriots head coach Mike Vrabel at an adults-only Arizona resort in April.  After ProFootballTalk first reported Russini has told people around the NFL that she is expecting to return to the industry next week, several options have emerged for what comes next. Russini is expected to launch new content sponsored by gambling operators, sources said, adding there have been talks with Jake Paul’s Betr platform, as well as offshore sportsbook BetUS. One source said Russini has also had talks with iHeart.  FOS discovered a new X/Twitter page titled “The Russini Report,” created in October, that has only three followers at the time of publication—Andres Vargas, a content creator and spokesperson for BetUS; Crossing Lines, a show sponsored by BetUS featuring ex-Barstool personality Jeff Nadu and cohost Donnie Seymour; and an additional video manager, named Natalie, following the page from a protected account. The “Russini Report” page indicates a show will air live on Wednesdays encompassing pro football news, injuries, and analysis. The page previously indicated the show would be “powered by BetUS,” but that language has since been edited out.  FOS screenshot On Friday, BetUS teased a new show on social media, with a woman making a comeback, launching Nov. 11. In the teaser, text messages reading, “Is it true? You back?” and “Football was weird without you reporting,” among others expressing similar sentiments pop up on the screen. Something big is arriving on November 11. 👀 Who do you think it is? 🔎 Only on BetUS. 🔥 Get the first look: https://t.co/WZZ5VL8HjU pic.twitter.com/LTwGDYkmce &mdash; BetUS Casino & Sportsbook (@BetUS_Official) November 6, 2026 In an announcement posted later Friday to its website, the company said: “BetUS is about to steal headlines after reaching an agreement with an NFL specialist with more than 10 years of experience in the game. “Although we can’t disclose the name just yet, we can reveal that this specialist will be hosting a live NFL betting show featuring game previews, matchup breakdowns, guest interviews, and data-backed NFL picks and predictions throughout the remainder of the 2026 season.” BetUS said it will announce the host’s name on Monday, and the first show will air on Wednesday at noon ET. The host will be “writing an accompanying column,” BetUS wrote, that “will also cover NFL matchups, detailing betting angles, breaking news, and industry rumors.” Basketball Hall of Famer Dennis Rodman is also an endorser of BetUS, which is headquartered in Costa Rica.  On Wednesday, Page Six reported Russini will be “headlining her own show” on a digital platform . “It’s been in the works for a while and she can’t wait to get back to what she loves,” a source told Page Six . Russini stopped responding to messages when presented with BetUS, Betr, and iHeart as places she has had talks with. Her agent, Matt Olson of CAA, referred FOS to the publicist Melissa Nathan, who declined to comment. Spokespeople for BetUS, Betr, and iHeart did not respond to requests for comment, nor did Vargas.  Jon “Stugotz” Weiner, a host for iHeart and its subsidiary Fox Sports Radio, has previously said he would welcome the chance to collaborate with Russini again. Russini was a frequent guest on Weiner’s previous show, The Dan Le Batard Show with Stugotz , and also joined Weiner on his God Bless Football show during Super Bowl week this past February. If Russini reaches a deal with iHeart, it is not expected to encompass Fox Sports Radio, one source said.  Barstool Sports founder Dave Portnoy also previously said he would consider hiring Russini, but he told FOS this week that she is not going to work for Barstool now. Sources told FOS that Kalshi, Underdog, and FanDuel are not in the running to sponsor her content at this time.  After Russini and Vrabel were photographed hugging and holding hands, the two issued statements saying their relationship was platonic, not romantic. Russini resigned from the New York Times –owned Athletic about a week later, and Vrabel missed one day of the NFL Draft to attend counseling.  One source told FOS he expects Russini to lean in to her NFL insider role. The question is how much cooperation she will get from NFL players and coaches, as well as the league itself, with the NFL playoffs and Super Bowl only months away. “All she needs is one big scoop to show she has motion,” says the source. FOS previously reported that Russini was planning a sports-media comeback . In September, The Athletic released an investigation saying Russini “should have disclosed to The Athletic the nature of her relationship with [Mike] Vrabel to allow editors to assess the kinds of stories on which she should have been working.” The investigation did not seek to discover whether the two had a romantic relationship, and while it noted Russini was “effusive” in her praise of Vrabel at times, it mirrored how he was covered by other outlets.  Ultimately, the investigation recommended editor’s notes be added to three videos and one story in which Russini covered Vrabel during her time at The Athletic . Email This field is for validation purposes and should be left unchanged. Sign up for the Tuned In Newsletter Get the latest sports media scoops & insights straight to your inbox once a week. Email (Required) Subscribe Δ This site is protected by reCAPTCHA and the Google Privacy Policy and Terms of Service apply. Share Tweet Facebook Linkedin Whatsapp Email Copy Link Link Copied Share Link Copied Matt Iseman takes viewers beyond the scoreboard, inside the deals, and face-to-face with the personalities shaping sports. Find Your Local Channel Latest Stories Opinion 27 minutes ago Is Rex Ryan Using ESPN to Campaign for a Cowboys Coaching Job? Stephen A. Smith has endorsed Ryan for the role multiple times. 1 hour ago NWSL Championship Shattering Ticket Records: ‘Perfect Storm’ With the Washington Spirit at home, ticket prices are soaring. 2 hours ago MLB Ramps Up Salary Cap Push With Fans Days Before Expected Lockout The league’s current labor deal expires on Dec. 1. What to Read Opinion by Michael McCarthy Is Rex Ryan Using ESPN to Campaign for a Cowboys Coaching Job? Stephen A. Smith has endorsed Ryan for the role multiple times. by Ben Horney Midterms Already Sending Ripples Through Prediction Markets “This has really been a bipartisan issue.” First at FOS by Mark Maske Four NFL Refs Pull Double Pay for Double Shift After Flight Chaos They worked games in Indianapolis and Cincinnati on the same day. by Margaret Fleming Gotham NWSL Title Defense Ends at Relocated NYC Semi NWSL players, fans, and coaches lamented this weekend’s stadium chaos. Front Office Sports Today 11/16/26 – NFL’s Second Half Storylines, WNBA’s JuJu Draft Lottery, Hutchinson Hurt Celebrating 1x 0:00 Featured Today by Mike Jakeman English Soccer Still Inundated by Gambling Ads Despite Jersey Ban The ban on front-of-shirt gambling ads looks strict, but it’s largely cosmetic. November 11, 2026 Mahjong Mania Has Come for College Sports Bespoke retailers are cashing in with officially licensed mahjong sets. November 6, 2026 Can MLS Capitalize on Son-Mania? Korea and Tottenham fans alike have flocked to LAFC’s away matches. November 4, 2026 High School Athletic Directors Say They’re Running ‘Mini Colleges’ ADs are managing a growing list of demands from athletes and parents. October 30, 2026 How Injuries Hit Pro Marathoners in the Wallet Staying healthy can get expensive—quickly. More in Media Exclusive by Ryan Glasspiegel , Mark Maske , and Michael McCarthy Dianna Russini Will Seek Credential for Super Bowl Radio Row Some NFL teams cite her association with BetUS as an issue. Exclusive November 12, 2026 NBA Doubles Down on Kenny Beecham, Enjoy Basketball Enjoy Basketball previously had a three-episode pilot deal with the league. November 13, 2026 FIA Bans F1 Journalist for Calling Driver a ‘Sneaky Little Bastard’ The journalist said he will be reinstated next year. Sponsored How Jeremy Bloom Is Reinventing the X Games Jeremy Bloom discusses transforming the X Games into a global brand. November 12, 2026 NFL Viewership Climbs As ESPN, Amazon Deliver Double-Digit Gains ESPN and Amazon have generated significant audience increases. November 11, 2026 Mike Tomlin Knows Coaching Talk Is ‘Going to Come Up’ Soon The ex-Steelers coach is “enjoying” his NBC Sports role. November 11, 2026 Russini’s Return Hits 45-Minute Delay, Sidesteps Vrabel Scandal Russini did joke about memes that have been made about her. November 11, 2026 NFL Network’s Europe Games Average a Record 6.9M in First ESPN Season NFL Network games this season also stream on the ESPN app. Privacy Manager",
