ctical Course Understand how local news can enhance and expand your strategy as a funder. This course will give you the context and tools you need to make effective investments in your community. Start Anytime Overview Start anytime with a self-directed course you can complete on your schedule Explore how philanthropy can support sustainable local news Identify funding opportunities and evaluate potential partners Hear real-world insights and case studies from funders Use a guided workbook to apply concepts to your organization Free to participants, supported by the Knight Foundation Ethically Funding Local News: A Practical Course quantity Enroll Now SKU: PKI-26 Tags: Funders , Funding , Local News , Megan Griffith-Greene , Philantrophy , Self-directed course Learning Outcomes By the end of this course, you will be able to: Understand what journalistic independence is and what it means to you as a funder Identify meaningful funding opportunities aligned with your mission and resources Navigate ethical conflicts that may come up Make the case for how independent local news can increase your impact on issues you care about Evaluate potential partners and make informed funding decisions Build trusted relationships, grounded in your values and the editorial independence of news organizations Free $ 0.00 Ethically Funding Local News: A Practical Course quantity Enroll Now Overview Start anytime with a self-directed course you can complete on your schedule Explore how philanthropy can support sustainable local news Identify funding opportunities and evaluate potential partners Hear real-world insights and case studies from funders Use a guided workbook to apply concepts to your organization Free to participants, supported by the Knight Foundation Training 5 or More People? get consulting & coaching Description Schedule Requirements How to support local journalism while protecting its independence Funding local news can strengthen communities. But knowing where and how to invest for the greatest impact isn’t always clear. Poynter’s self-directed course for funders gives you a clear, practical introduction to local news and the role philanthropy can play in strengthening it. Whether you’re new to funding local news or exploring how it connects to your organization’s priorities, this course helps you move from curiosity to confident action. Led by Poynter faculty member Megan Griffith-Greene, you’ll learn how local news works today, why it matters and how it is evolving. You’ll explore how to identify meaningful funding opportunities, evaluate potential partners and support work that makes a measurable difference in the communities you serve. Along the way, you’ll hear directly from funders and unpack real-world examples, so you can understand not just what works, but why.     Why this matters Communities rely on accurate, trustworthy information to function and stay connected. But local news is under pressure, and funders play a critical role in helping it evolve and thrive. This course gives you the tools to make informed funding decisions, connect your mission to community information needs and support sustainable, ethical journalism. How it works This is a self-directed, online course you can complete on your own time. Through short video lessons featuring Poynter faculty and experienced funders, you’ll explore key concepts and real-world examples. Quick activities encourage you to think creatively about new concepts. And a downloadable workbook helps you apply what you’re learning and build a strategy tailored to your organization. By the end, you’ll have practical tools and a clearer path forward. Offered free to participants thanks to support from the Knight Foundation and with help from Press Forward, this course is part of Poynter’s broader effort to strengthen local news and the funders behind it. Looking for more? Explore additional ways to deepen your impact: Live workshops for funders – Interactive sessions designed for real-world application Custom consulting and facilitation – Tailored support for your organization’s goals Free tools and resources – Explore our free tools for funders Understand local news. Fund it effectively. Strengthen your impact. Questions? If you need assistance, email us at info@poynter.org . On-demand The entire course should take about an hour and a half to complete . You can work on this course in more than one sitting; your progress will be saved so it’s easy to pick up where you left off. By the end, you’ll have practical tools and a clearer path forward. Who should enroll? This course is designed for funders, foundation leaders and program staff who want to better understand local news and how to support it effectively. Whether you’re new to funding local news or exploring how it connects to your organization’s priorities, this course will help you make informed, strategic decisions. Cost Free to participants, thanks to support from the Knight Foundation. Instructors Megan Griffith-Greene Faculty Megan Griffith-Greene is a faculty member at Poynter whose work focuses on strategy, leadership and ethics. She works with media companies across the globe on... Read More Thank you to our sponsor With help from \"> Related Products Sale Online Beyond the Inverted Pyramid: Creating Alternative Story Forms $ 50.00 Original price was: $50.00. $ 0.00 Current price is: $0.00. Certificate Online Getting It Right: Accuracy and Verification in the Digital Age $ 50.00 Sale Online How Any Journalist Can Earn Trust (International Edition) $ 50.00 Original price was: $50.00. $ 0.00 Current price is: $0.00. Sale Online How to Spot Misinformation Online $ 50.00 Original price was: $50.00. $ 0.00 Current price is: $0.00.",
