      "contentType": "text/html; charset=utf-8",
      "pageTitle": "Phil Collins Knew He Was ‘Lucky,’ if Not Cool",
      "pageDescription": "When the Rock & Roll Hall of Fame inducts Collins as a solo artist this month, it will recognize a hitmaker whose popularity often overshadowed his intensity.",
      "evidenceText": "Phil Collins Knew He Was ‘Lucky,’ if Not Cool - The New York Times Music | Phil Collins Knew He Was ‘Lucky,’ if Not Cool https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/08/arts/music/phil-collins-rock-roll-hall-of-fame.html Share full article 268 You have a preview view of this article while we are checking your access. When we have confirmed access, the full article content will load. Phil Collins Knew He Was ‘Lucky,’ if Not Cool When the Rock & Roll Hall of Fame inducts Collins as a solo artist this month, it will recognize a hitmaker whose popularity often overshadowed his intensity. Credit... Supported by SKIP ADVERTISEMENT Listen · 12:12 min Share full article 268 By Jon Pareles Visuals by Dana Scruggs Nov. 8, 2026 Phil Collins considers himself “lucky.” The English drummer, songwriter and frontman has been a child actor, a versatile sideman, a progressive-rock innovator with Genesis, a skillful producer and a prolific pop hitmaker. He has also gotten divorced three times, been mocked as uncool, struggled with alcoholism, and been rediscovered through sampling and nostalgia. Like all long rock careers, his has been complicated. On Nov. 14, Collins, 75, will be inducted into the Rock & Roll Hall of Fame for the second time. The first, in 2010, was as a member of Genesis . Now he’s being recognized for his extensive, and hugely popular, solo work. With the renewed recognition, Collins has re-emerged to burnish and reassess his musical legacy, even though his performing years may well be over. “I’d gotten quite used to being overlooked,” he said in an interview at his Manhattan apartment. Photographs of his five children filled a coffee table. On the windowsill sat a Diamond Award trophy, presented by the Recording Industry Association of America when his 1985 album, “No Jacket Required,” surpassed 10 million sales. Alongside it, an electric keyboard faced a view of Central Park. Image “I’d gotten quite used to being overlooked,” Collins said, despite a career in which he sold over 100 million albums as a solo artist and another 100 million as a member of the band Genesis. “I didn’t go looking to see who was nominated this year, so I was quite surprised when I heard I was up for induction,” he continued. “It’s just a pat on the back, you know. If it wasn’t there, I wouldn’t be craving it. But it is there, and I’m very, very pleased.” By the late 1980s, Collins was nearly ubiquitous, his music was such a radio staple that some began to dismiss him as anodyne easy listening, ignoring the emotional and rhythmic intensity of the songs themselves. Thank you for your patience while we verify access. If you are in Reader mode please exit and log into your Times account, or subscribe for all of The Times. Thank you for your patience while we verify access. Already a subscriber? Log in . Want all of The Times? Subscribe . Related Content Advertisement SKIP ADVERTISEMENT",
