      "pageTitle": "Variety",
      "pageDescription": "Sen. Cory Booker, Democrat of New Jersey, is holding a &quot;spotlight hearing&quot; in Washington about Paramount Skydance&#039;s proposed $111 billion deal to acquire Warner Bros. Discovery. David Ellison, Paramount&#039;s chairman and CEO, will not be able to attend the hearing because of a death in the family, according to the company.",
      "evidenceText": "Paramount CEO David Ellison Can't Attend Senate Hearing on Warner Bros. Merger Because of Death in the Family Paramount CEO David Ellison Can't Attend Senate Hearing on Warner Bros. Merger Because of Death in the Family &times; Continue to Variety SKIP AD You will be redirected back to your article in seconds Breaking News Robert Pattinson Is the New James Bond Apr 15, 2026 5:51am PT Paramount CEO David Ellison Can’t Attend Senate Hearing on Warner Bros. Merger Because of Death in the Family, Company Says By Todd Spangler Plus Icon Todd Spangler NY Digital Editor xpangler Latest Trump Rages That ‘60 Minutes’ Current Leadership Is ‘Even Worse Than Their Predecessors,’ Claims Newsmagazine’s Iran Whistleblower Report Lacked Context 3 hours ago Apple Podcasts Are Coming to Apple TV App 4 hours ago Microsoft Apologizes for Massive Outlook Outage, Affected Customers Can Request Credits for Service Interruption 3 days ago See All Add us as a Google Preferred Source Alberto E. Rodriguez/Getty Images for CinemaCon Sen. Cory Booker, Democrat of New Jersey, is holding a “spotlight hearing” Wednesday in Washington about Paramount Skydance ’s proposed $111 billion deal to acquire Warner Bros. Discovery . David Ellison , Paramount’s chairman and CEO, will not be able to attend the hearing because of a death in the family, according to the company. “Regretfully, Mr. Ellison is unable to be in Washington D.C. on Wednesday, as he is attending a funeral due to a death in the family,” Ted Lehman, Paramount Skydance’s SVP and head U.S. public policy and government affairs, wrote in a letter to Booker dated April 14. Booker had invited Ellison to speak at the hearing in a letter sent Monday. Related Stories Japan IP Market to Return to Cannes in 2027 as Tokyo Partnership Continues A Paramount Skydance rep declined to identify which member of the Ellison family had died. According to Lehman, Paramount’s megadeal to merge with WBD “is quite straightforward: We believe the transaction should be reviewed on the merits. And on the merits, the transaction is procompetitive.” Lehman continued, “Most importantly, a Paramount Skydance/Warner Bros. Discovery transaction is all about increasing the amount of content released in theaters and streaming platforms. This increase in content will create competition and greater demand for creative talent. We want customers in theaters, where they can experience the full moviegoing experience, and the increased number of theatrical releases that the combined company will generate will provide our streaming platform with an impressive array of content that will check the dominance of rivals like Netflix.” Ellison had previously declined an invitation to testify before the Senate Judiciary Committee’s Subcommittee on Antitrust, Competition Policy and Consumer Rights. The subcommittee held a hearing Feb. 3 where Netflix co-CEO Ted Sarandos  and Warner Bros. Discovery chief revenue and strategy officer Bruce Campbell testified amid sharp questioning. Following the hearing, Booker requested Ellison’s written testimony, “seeking insights on the proposed sale of Warner Bros. Discovery and Paramount’s parallel bid, and asking Ellison to address concerns about political interference in the merger review process,” according to a group of eight Democratic senators, including Booker. However, they said, “Ellison’s response failed to address these issues.” Those included questions about whether Ellison had ever donated money to Trump directly or indirectly and whether there had been any communications regarding a personal benefit to Trump with a potential Paramount-WBD merger. The senators also said the Feb. 19 letter served “as notice to preserve records related to the proposed Paramount-Warner Bros. Discovery transaction.” According to Booker’s letter this week, Paramount-Skydance has not responded to preservation letter signed by the eight U.S. senators. “As the leader of the company seeking to execute one of the largest media mergers in American history, your continued unwillingness to engage with Congressional oversight is itself a matter of public concern,” Booker wrote. Paramount has said the Warner Bros. Discovery merger would ensure creatives have “more avenues for their work, not fewer” and that the combined company would “greenlight more projects” and “back bold ideas.” Per Booker’s April 13 letter, “These are serious commitments. This forum is an opportunity for you to make them directly to Congress and to the workers, journalists, and creators whose livelihoods depend on whether those promises are kept.” Booker’s letter to Ellison also cited an open letter now signed by more than 2,000 Hollywood professionals as of Tuesday opposing the Paramount-WBD deal, saying in part that “The integrity, independence, and diversity of our industry would be grievously compromised” as a result of the merger. Signatories to the letter, available at BlocktheMerger.com , include Kristen Stewart, Ben Stiller, Florence Pugh, Pedro Pascal, Edward Norton, Atsuko Okatsuka and Joaquin Phoenix. The Senate hearing is slated for Wednesday starting at at 3 p.m. ET. Speakers scheduled to appear are: documentary filmmaker David Borenstein; Michael Isaac, director of legal services at the Writers Guild of America East; Katie Phang, attorney, legal analyst and former host of “The Katie Phang Show”; and Mara Verheyden-Hilliard, co-founder and executive director of the Partnership for Civil Justice Fund and member of the steering committee of Jane Fonda’s Committee for the First Amendment. In addition, actor Mark Ruffalo — who has strongly opposed the Paramount-WBD merger — will testify via videoconference at the hearing, according to Booker’s office. Paramount’s proposed Warner Bros. takeover is subject to approval by WBD shareholders at an April 23 special meeting. It also is pending regulatory approval. Last week, Paramount confirmed it brought on the  sovereign wealth funds of Saudi Arabia, Qatar and Abu Dhabi,  as well as LionTree Investment Fund, as equity investors in connection with the WBD takeover bid. In aggregate, the  three Middle Eastern funds are investing close to $24 billion , with Saudi Arabia’s Public Investment Fund taking a roughly $10 billion stake. Jump to Comments ‘Lady’ Review: The Vibrant Street Life of Lagos Energizes a Rough-Edged Character Study ‘Hold Onto Me’ Director Myrsini Aristidou Kicks Off Historic Oscar Campaign for Sundance Audience Award Winner: ‘I Really Feel Like We’re Underdogs’ Loading comments... Most Popular Robert Pattinson Is Officially James Bond: Meet the New 007 Actor Grammy Nominations 2027: Raye Leads With 11 Noms as Rosalía, Olivia Rodrigo, Ella Langley and Other Female Artists Dominate Willie Geist Will Exit MS NOW’s ‘Morning Joe’ After Nearly 20 Years for Expanded Role at NBC’s ‘Today’ Billy Evans Addresses Amanda Seyfried's Portrayal of Elizabeth Holmes in ‘You Can See Everything': She Was 'Eager' to 'Play a Character' Grammys Snubs and Surprises: Harry Styles Shut Out, Raye’s Huge Year and Noah Kahan Only Gets One Nomination Phoebe Bridgers’ Skeletal Transformation on ‘SNL’ Wasn’t Just a Startling Special Effect &mdash; It Emotionally Elevated One of the Best Pop Songs Ever&hellip; 'Slow Horses' Renewed for Seasons 9 and 10 but Jack Lowden's Return Is Uncertain Johnny Depp’s ‘Ebenezer,’ ‘Ramayana’ Dominate Global Box Office as ‘Godzilla Minus Zero’ Crosses $100&hellip; Beyoncé Makes Grammys History as First Artist With 100 Nominations Hoobastank, Simple Plan and More Bands Performed Mid-Flight During Nationwide Air Travel Ground Stop Must Read Film Skydance Succession: As David Ellison Sets His Team, Heads Are Rolling as Hollywood Finds Out Who’s Really in Charge By Andrew Wallenstein 5 days Film Sex, Love and War: How the Racy Gay Epic 'La Bola Negra' Could Win Netflix's First Best Picture Oscar Film Ben Affleck Explains It All: Artists Equity, the Death of Movie Stars and the Dilemma With Co