      "id": "a0e3d33597923ff0ac63a994da64b8b55f63725ac8c148d078911eac1f700acf",
      "title": "Misread Mandate",
      "url": "https://www.status.news/p/karen-attiah-washington-post-settlement-charlie-kirk",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-16T18:39:59.178Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://www.status.news/p/karen-attiah-washington-post-settlement-charlie-kirk",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=utf-8",
      "pageTitle": "Karen Attiah's Post-Script",
      "pageDescription": "After winning her fight to be reinstated at The Post, the former opinion columnist tells Status why she ultimately decided she couldn&#x27;t return to the Jeff Bezos-owned newspaper.",
      "evidenceText": "Karen Attiah's Post-Script Karen Attiah's Post-Script After winning her fight to be reinstated at The Post, the former opinion columnist tells Status why she ultimately decided she couldn't return to the Jeff Bezos-owned newspaper. Natalie Korach Nov 8, 2026 Karen Attiah's Post-Script After winning her fight to be reinstated at The Post, the former opinion columnist tells Status why she ultimately decided she couldn't return to the Jeff Bezos-owned newspaper. Natalie Korach Nov 8, 2026 On Thursday, Karen Attiah posed a question to her more than 600,000 social media followers. “Should I return to The Washington Post ?” the former opinion columnist wrote, asking users to vote yes or no. The post served as an especially cryptic reminder of the unusual situation at the Jeff Bezos -owned paper: Nearly three months after an independent arbitrator ordered the publication to reinstate Attiah and award her full back pay after wrongly firing her last year, there was still no resolution to the process. As of Sunday, there is finally an answer: Attiah won’t be returning to The Post. A spokesperson for the Washington-Baltimore News Guild confirmed to Status that it reached a settlement with the newspaper, bringing an end to the months-long dispute over her reinstatement . Attiah will not rejoin The Post in any capacity, though part of the settlement includes back pay ordered by the arbitrator, the union told me. The remaining settlement terms were not disclosed. “There was still a chance to go back as of Thursday,” Attiah told Status of her decision. “I think I just didn’t feel safe returning and that I would be protected. It’s not just a job. It’s also about whether I could be able to do the journalism that I not only wanted to do, but that I think the readers deserved.” Attiah also mused about whether Democrats’ electoral romp on Tuesday would prompt a cultural shift at the paper. “There’s always going to be a part of me that wonders, especially after seeing the midterm election results, about the alternative path if I could have done it,” she added. The Post didn’t respond to a request for comment on the decision, nor did it respond to repeated inquiries from Status about the situation in the months since the arbitrator’s ruling. But the resolution closes a particularly contentious chapter for the paper, which had remained publicly silent about Attiah's reinstatement since the arbitration ruling in August. While The Post previously said in an unsigned statement it would respect the arbitration process, it had offered no public update on whether it intended to bring the columnist back or award her the back pay. Behind the scenes, however, the union had remained engaged in negotiations with the newspaper over her future. Attiah, of course, was hastily fired last year amid the backlash following the killing of Charlie Kirk , after she posted comments on social media about violence in America that didn’t explicitly name the right-wing activist. Post executives at the time, including former chief executive and publisher Will Lewis and then-opinion editor Adam O’Neal , fired her for “misconduct” without asking her to delete the posts first or giving her a chance to discuss the decision. A year later, the arbitration ruling came down in Attiah’s favor, as the arbitrator said the paper did not have “good and sufficient cause” to fire her. Attiah, a veteran opinion columnist who had served as Global Opinions editor at the time of Jamal Khashoggi’s murder, had initially been hopeful to return to her perch at the storied newspaper. But she made clear, in a conversation with me shortly after the ruling was handed down in August, that she needed certain assurances to do so. Specifically, she wanted to know whether she would be permitted to write freely under the paper's leadership. Ultimately, she said, she did not get those assurances. “While I remain proud of the victory, I came to the painful realization that returning to The Washington Post would come without the guaranteed freedom and institutional support to do what I loved: use my voice, seek truth, and serve readers,” Attiah wrote in a post on Substack announcing her decision on Sunday. Indeed, the opinion section Attiah would return to is already undergoing another transition. O'Neal, the editor who helped orchestrate her firing and oversaw the section's rightward shift under Bezos’ ownership, has since departed the newspaper. During his tenure, The Post laid off opinion staffers, brought in more conservative voices, and invested in culture-war podcasts and video programming, part of a broader overhaul that alienated many inside the newsroom. As Status previously reported based on audio we obtained, weeks before Attiah’s firing, O’Neal told her in a private meeting that her writing and social media presence were not consistent with his vision for the section. Attiah later characterized the meeting as evidence that the newspaper had been looking for a reason to push her out when it fired her. “I still wish there were more answers about what exactly went down,” Attiah told Status . “But I tried to fight the good fight.” The section is now being overseen by Mark Lasswell on an interim basis, though some internally believe he could be given the job permanently. It wouldn’t be the first time The Post would quietly make such a move: The paper recently installed Jeff D'Onofrio as its permanent publisher after he initially assumed the position on an interim basis. But the newspaper has not publicly indicated whether Lasswell will remain in the role or how closely the section will adhere to the ideological direction established under O'Neal. In any case, multiple people familiar with the situation have suggested to me that The Post was not exactly eager to bring Attiah back into the fold. But the opinion desk’s transformation also raises questions about which voices The Post has deemed acceptable under its new leadership. One person familiar with the situation questioned whether Attiah's political views were meaningfully different from those of other columnists, including Dana Milbank and David Ignatius , who remained at the paper. The person also pointed to the dramatic decline in Black representation on the opinion pages, from six columnists to one, as a troubling development for the section. For Attiah, the settlement brings an end to a painful fight with an institution where she spent more than a decade building her career. And she told me she is still coming to terms with the “consequential decision,” describing her emotions as “still raw.” The latest episode of Power Lines is out. In this week’s episode: We examine the fallout from Democrats’ blue tsunami in the midterms, as Donald Trump and Rupert Murdoch blame one another for the GOP’s bloodbath. Plus, we break down what the results mean for some of Trump’s billionaire pals, like David Ellison , Elon Musk , and Mark Zuckerberg . Finally, was the media afraid to say what was really happening in the lead-up to Election Day ? We discuss why coverage of the coming Democratic sweep didn’t seem to come through, even as the data and the context were all pointing in one direction. You can watch on YouTube—or listen on Apple Podcasts , Spotify , or wherever you get your podcasts . If you enjoy the program, subscribe so you never miss an episode! Dana White and Donald Trump pose for a photo during the UFC 309 event. (Photo by Sarah Stier/Getty Images) Huh?: Donald Trump posted a bizarre plea on Truth Social on Saturday, offering the job of White House press secretary to … UFC boss Dana White . [ CNN ] White turned down the offer, Kaitlan Collins and Isabelle D’Antonio reported. He also deflected a question about Trump’s post at a news conference, saying that he would meet with the president in Washington on Monday but noting he has a “lot of jobs already.” “60 Minutes” teased correspondent Ross Douthat’s interview with Trump, saying it found him “reflective and seemingly accepting of his party’s midterm defeat.” [ Bluesky ] In one clip, Douthat asked Trump how his net worth had ballooned during his presidency, according to a Forbes estimate. Trump’s answer prompted Dan Alexander , the Forbes editor who calculates these net worths, to call it “nonsense.” [ Mediaite ] Bari Weiss’ The Free Press retracted and deleted a social media post and video from reporter Maya Sulkin that falsely claimed Abdul El-Sayed “gave his victory speech in Arabic.” The publication acknowledged, “The post contained a factual error and its tone fell short of what we believe advances healthy public discourse.” The retraction came two days after the publication had issued a correction, but left up the original post and video. On Sunday, it said the “rigorous editorial process that produces the work that appears on our website and in our newsletters must apply to everything we post on social media. We’ll be updating how we do things to achieve just that.” Attorney General Todd Blanche ousted the Justice Department’s top national security official, John Eisenberg , after clashing over the pace of media-leak investigations, Ben Penn reported. [ Bloomberg Law ] New White House chief of staff Stephen Miller has been said to favor arresting journalists in an attempt to pressure them to reveal confidential sources. [ The Atlantic ] Uh oh!: Former Fox News host turned Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth could face impeachment in the House as soon as this week, as he continues to take blame for the GOP’s election rout. [ Axios ] As part of its deal to acquire Roku , Fox Corporation committed to keep carrying non-Fox content on Roku for at least three years and to “not discriminate against non-Fox content” in search results. [ Variety ] Right-wing extremist Laura Loomer , a top Trump ally, was accused of being paid thousands for political social-media posts without disclosing the arrangements, Drew Harwell and Scott Nover reported. [ WaPo ] Popular progressive podcasters Jennifer Welch and Angie Sullivan apologized for laughing at an “insensitive joke” about Charlie Kirk after at least one sponsor said it would no longer advertise on their show “I’ve Had It.” [ THR ] Changing of the guard?: Ron DeSantis called for a “big change” in “conservative media culture,” dubbing much of it a “grift.” [ Mediaite ] Bill O’Reilly and pro- MAGA radio host Sid Rosenberg called JD Vance “disloyal” after he blamed Republicans’ midterm trouncing on the Iran War . [ Mediaite ] Surprise!: Robert Pattinson screen-tested for the role of James Bond , apparently joining hopefuls Callum Turner , Josh O’Connor , and Jack Lowden . Nicholas Hoult has also reportedly emerged as a contender. [ THR ] James Talarico was an unexpected guest during the “Saturday Night Live” cold open. [ Variety ] Adam Driver said he wouldn’t reprise his role in the HBO reboot of Lena Dunham’s “Girls.” [ Cinema Blend ] A still from \"Godzilla Minus Zero.\" (Screenshot via YouTube/Toho Studios) King of the box office! : “Godzilla Minus Zero” roared to the weekend domestic crown, grabbing $31 million in its debut. The tally represented the largest opening for a live-action foreign-language film in North America , beating out 2002’s “Hero.” The sequel to 2023’s “Godzilla Minus One” also scored big with IMAX theatergoers, with its screens contributing about 40% of its domestic haul. Warner Bros.’ animated adaptation of “Cat in the Hat,” which scored poorly with critics but was better received by audiences, came in second with around $20 million. The Bill Hader -starred film added just around $10 million from international markets, making it another potential flop for Warner after duds including “Digger” and “Supergirl” this year. “Drummer Boy,” a Christian musical, placed third for the weekend, nabbing around $9.3 million. A24’s “You Can See Everything,” the Elizabeth Holmes documentary from Nathan Fielder and Lance Oppenheim , held strong in fourth place, bringing in another $8.8 million and boosting its domestic haul to nearly $80 million. It’s now the second-highest-grossing non-music documentary of all time, behind just Michael Moore’s “Fahrenheit 9/11.” The Indian epic “Ramayana: Rise of a Legend” came in fifth, earning about $8.6 million, but it brought in over $55 million globally. [ Data via Box Office Mojo ] Keep reading VIEW MORE About Us Archive Go Premium Quick Links Home Power Lines Archive Shop Gift About Us Advertise With Us Authors Oliver Darcy Jon Passantino Natalie Korach Brian Lowry Status is the definitive nightly briefing that cuts through the noise to inform readers about what is really happening in the corridors of media power. <div class=\"_1lgh8460 flex flex-col gap-2\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:400px;--gap:0px;--input-background-color:inherit;--input-border-color:transparent;--input-checkbox-radius:0px;--input-text-color:#030712;--input-label-color:#030712;--input-border-width:1px;--input-border-radius:0px;--input-radio-checkbox-accent-color:#030712;--input-radio-checkbox-background-color:#F9FAFB;--gdpr-text-color:#030712;--gdpr-checkbox-color:#030712;--input-font-family:\"Slate\", system-ui, sans-serif;--button-font-family:\"Open Sans\", system-ui, sans-serif;--button-icon-side:right;--button-icon-col",
