      "id": "aef2e26799ddab6a3ae98a6269d6ae57eadf235d8b6722fa85c2766b4b9443e3",
      "title": "Misread Mandate",
      "url": "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2026/nov/06/prince-harry-others-appeal-daily-mail-phone-hacking-claim",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-16T18:39:59.178Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2026/nov/06/prince-harry-others-appeal-daily-mail-phone-hacking-claim",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "Prince Harry and others seek to challenge ruling in Daily Mail privacy case",
      "pageDescription": "Group including Doreen Lawrence and Elton John face &#xA3;50m bill after losing claims over alleged unlawful information gathering",
      "evidenceText": "Prince Harry and others seek to challenge ruling in Daily Mail privacy case | Prince Harry | The Guardian Skip to main content Skip to navigation Harry arriving at the high court in London in January. Photograph: Kirsty Wigglesworth/AP View image in fullscreen Harry arriving at the high court in London in January. Photograph: Kirsty Wigglesworth/AP Prince Harry Prince Harry and others seek to challenge ruling in Daily Mail privacy case Group including Doreen Lawrence and Elton John face £50m bill after losing claims over alleged unlawful information gathering Matthew Weaver Fri 6 Nov 2026 13.59 EST Last modified on Fri 6 Nov 2026 15.01 EST Share Prefer the Guardian on Google Prince Harry and five other prominent figures have launched a legal challenge against a judge’s verdict in their unsuccessful claims against the Daily Mail’s publisher, Associated Newspapers . Harry, Doreen Lawrence, the mother of the murdered black teenager Stephen Lawrence, the singer Elton John and his husband, David Furnish, and the actors Elizabeth Hurley and Sadie Frost faced a legal bill of more than £50m after losing their case claiming that Mail journalists used unlawful methods to source stories. In an unequivocal ruling in July, Mr Justice Nicklin dismissed their case, stating that the claimants had not proved any information had been obtained unlawfully. His 436-page written verdict, after an 11-week trial, said it could not be inferred that a story had been obtained unlawfully if there remained a legitimate and realistic legal way in which it could have been sourced. In a statement issued on Friday, a spokesperson for Harry and his five fellow claimants said: “We confirm that the claimants have today filed applications with the court of appeal for permission to appeal the judgments of Mr Justice Nicklin in respect of his dismissal of their claims and the indemnity costs order. “The claimants respectfully disagree with those unprecedented decisions, which they consider to be legally and factually flawed.” Court staff confirmed on Friday that an application to appeal against the rulings had been filed at the court of appeal. Barristers for the claimants had alleged during the trial that private investigators, freelance journalists and staff at Associated Newspapers had carried out acts including voicemail interception, landline tapping and obtaining information by deception, also known as “blagging”. But lawyers for the publisher said Daily Mail and Mail on Sunday journalists had provided a “compelling account of a pattern of legitimate sourcing of articles”, including friends and “leaky” social circles, press officers and spokespeople, as well as previous reporting, freelance journalists and stories from other newspapers and news agencies. Associated’s legal team described the claims as “lurid” and “preposterous”. skip past newsletter promotion Free newsletter | Average of 2-3 times a day Sign up to Breaking News UK Sign up for the Breaking News UK email to get newsletter alerts direct to your inbox Enter your email Sign up after newsletter promotion After the verdict the former Mail editor Paul Dacre said the case was a “conspiracy” orchestrated by press regulation campaigners to “destroy a paper”. Harry and Lawrence said the verdict was a “complete and obvious whitewash”. They added: “When the court says there is not sufficient evidence of wrongdoing … then one does wonder how justice was ever going to be achieved.” In August, Nicklin ruled that the claimants had to pay £9,544,355 within seven days as an initial contribution to the publisher’s costs before further hearings to determine the total sum. Explore more on these topics Prince Harry Associated Newspapers Daily Mail Doreen Lawrence Elton John Sadie Frost David Furnish news Share Reuse this content More on this story More on this story Prince Harry and others ordered to pay interim £9.5m to Daily Mail publisher Prince Harry and co-claimants face huge legal bill due to insurance shortfall The prince and the ‘professional liar’: inside Harry’s battle against the Daily Mail Doreen Lawrence will not foot any of legal bill for failed attempt to sue Daily Mail After losing to the Mail, Prince Harry seems doomed to a sad life in California. And he did it to himself An emphatic victory for the Mail: how a £50m privacy case unravelled in court Prince Harry and other claimants could face £50m legal bill after losing phone-hacking case Prince Harry gets burned in his mission to ‘slay dragons’ of British media Daily Mail’s ‘aggressive’ reporting on Prince Harry and Meghan ‘irreparably damaged’ press briefings An angry prince and leaky friends: key moments in the Daily Mail hacking trial Most viewed Most viewed UK UK politics Education Media Society Law Scotland Wales Northern Ireland",
