      "pageTitle": "Trump Asks Dana White of U.F.C. to Be His Press Secretary",
      "pageDescription": "On social media, the president offered the now-vacant job of White House press secretary to Dana White, the organization’s C.E.O., and told him to bring along fighters as aides.",
      "evidenceText": "Trump Asks Dana White of U.F.C. to Be His Press Secretary - The New York Times Advertisement SKIP ADVERTISEMENT You have a preview view of this article while we are checking your access. When we have confirmed access, the full article content will load. President Trump said on Saturday that he had offered Dana White, the chief executive of the Ultimate Fighting Championship and a longtime friend, the role of White House press secretary. Mr. Trump made the announcement in a social media post in which he pleaded with Mr. White — a “violent Fighter and Warrior” — to take the job. The president said he had also requested that Mr. White bring with him a number of U.F.C. fighters to serve as aides who could respond to attacks against the administration. “I have requested that he bring with him assistants, even though some are not from our Country, of Islam Makhachev, Khabib Nurmagomedov, Justin Gaethje, Daniel Cormier, Amanda Nunes, and Kayla Harris,” Mr. Trump wrote. “We need these people to defend us against the Lunatic Left. Dana will be a Press Secretary like none other. Dana, please take the job!” It was unclear if Mr. Trump’s out-of-the-blue offer to Mr. White was meant to be a joke or was a serious offer aimed at projecting defiance after his midterm election losses. The White House did not immediately respond to a request for comment. A U.F.C. spokeswoman did not immediately respond to a request for comment. Mr. White addressed the announcement during a post-fight news conference in London on Saturday , but declined to say whether he and the president had discussed the offer. He said he was flying to Washington to meet with Mr. Trump. Thank you for your patience while we verify access. If you are in Reader mode please exit and log into your Times account, or subscribe for all of The Times. Thank you for your patience while we verify access. Already a subscriber? Log in . Want all of The Times? Subscribe . Related Content Advertisement SKIP A