      "finalUrl": "https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7765656/2026/11/09/college-football-playoff-24-teams-espn-expansion/",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=utf-8",
      "pageTitle": "ESPN, College Football Playoff announce details of expansion to 24 teams: Here’s how it works",
      "pageDescription": "This season will be the last with a 12-team College Football Playoff, as expansion to 24 teams will arrive in the 2027 season.",
      "evidenceText": "ESPN, College Football Playoff announce details of expansion to 24 teams: Here’s how it works - The Athletic College Football Playoff Expansion Case For Success 24-Team Plan Approved Inside Playoff Plan's Journey Bowl System Changes Winners and Losers ESPN, College Football Playoff announce details of expansion to 24 teams: Here’s how it works College football's postseason tournament is doubling in size starting in 2027. Kevin C. Cox / Getty Images By Stewart Mandel and Ralph D. Russo Nov. 9, 2026 Updated Nov. 15, 2026 Share article 1K The College Football Playoff’s expansion to 24 teams is now a reality, a historic and dramatic shift that has already begun changing almost everything about the sport — with one notable exception. ESPN will remain the exclusive rights holder through 2031-32 after the CFP and the network announced the long-anticipated news Monday. Advertisement The sides came to a preliminary agreement last month, and then it was just a matter of hammering out contract details that altered existing deals between the conferences and the network. According to a source briefed on the new contract but not authorized to speak publicly about the details, the deal is worth $1.7 billion over five years, with an average annual value of $345 million per year. However, ESPN will also be repaid around $140 million per year for the canceled ACC, SEC and Big 12 championship games it owns rights to through separate agreements. The new deal will replace the existing six-year, $1.3 billion contract ESPN and the CFP agreed to in 2024 when the Playoff expanded to 12 teams. “At its core, the decision was driven by a desire to increase opportunities for FBS programs, student-athletes and fanbases to participate in the Playoff and have a chance to play for the CFP national championship,” CFP executive director Rich Clark said on a teleconference with reporters. “Simply put, more opportunity.” To make room on the calendar for a new round of Playoff games, the 10 FBS conferences were expected to end league championship games that are always played on the first weekend of December. Minutes after the CFP and ESPN expansion announcement, the SEC was the first conference to say it was discontinuing its championship game after 35 years. Similar announcements from the ACC, Big 12 and Big Ten soon followed . The move from 12 teams to 24 marks a monumental change for college football that will place more emphasis than ever on the postseason to determine a national champion and is expected to have sweeping ramifications. The CFP was still a four-team event as recently as 2023 and will stage the third and final 12-team Playoff this season. The 24-team field will be made up of four guaranteed spots for Power 4 conference champions (ACC, Big Ten, Big 12 and SEC), one guaranteed spot for the highest-ranked team from the Group of 6 leagues (American, Conference USA, MAC, Mountain West, Pac-12 and Sun Belt) and 19 at-large spots chosen by the selection committee. Seeds 1-8 will earn a first-round bye and host a second-round game on campus. Seeds 9-24 will play first-round games on the campus of the higher seed. Advertisement Clark said the plan as of now is to continue ESPN’s weekly rankings show that has been airing on Tuesdays in November. Why the College Football Playoff is expanding to 24 teams Stewart Mandel The first week of December will become an off week for CFP teams and the new date for the Army-Navy game. For next year, the Heisman Trophy ceremony will also move up a week and be handed out on the weekend of Dec. 3-5. An exact date is to be determined. First-round games will begin the second weekend of December on campuses from Thursday through Saturday. Second-round games will take place the following weekend, also on campuses from Thursday through Saturday, giving the top seeds a chance to host a Playoff game. Quarterfinals will continue to be played around New Year’s Eve and New Year’s Day at bowl sites, and the semifinals and final will remain in their scheduled sites and dates at bowl and neutral venues. The final 12-team Playoff national championship will be played Jan. 25, 2027, in Las Vegas. The first 24-team title game will be Jan. 24, 2028, in New Orleans. The expanded Playoff comes with a new set of standards aimed at making schedules more uniform across conferences. Teams in Power 4 conferences will be expected to play at least 10 games against conference opponents, teams from other P4 leagues or Notre Dame. Group of 6 teams will be expected to play at least 10 games against conference opponents, P4 schools, schools from other G6 leagues or independent FBS schools. The drastic expansion of the Playoff could spell doom for many of the other 35 bowl games outside of the event. Though the holiday-week bowls remain popular with TV viewers — 8.7 million people watched the 2025 Pop-Tarts Bowl between BYU and Georgia Tech — the games had been losing prestige for years, as star players routinely opted out of them. After getting left out of the CFP last year, Notre Dame declined to play in a bowl entirely. Advertisement “We certainly feel there remains a need for postseason opportunities for teams (beyond the CFP),” said Nick Dawson, ESPN’s senior vice president for college sports programming and acquisitions. “There are clearly still good TV windows to be had as well.” Critics also fear the 24-team Playoff will devalue college football’s high-stakes regular season. Based on the selection committee’s rankings in previous seasons , nearly half the field will consist of 9-3 or even 8-4 teams, the majority of them from the Big Ten and SEC. What You Should Read Next Who would have made a 24-team field in the College Football Playoff era? Try 80 different teams Eighty teams would have made a 24-team CFP over the last 12 seasons. Here's what we learned from mocking 12 years of brackets. “We’re aware of the fan sentiment and that many fans aren’t in favor of expansion , but that was certainly weighed into the decision,” Clark said. “It was considered, but ultimately, we just need to make a spectacular Playoff. The CFP has a responsibility to our fans. They’re the lifeblood of college football, and we owe it to them to make this great, and that really is our intent.” The 24-team CFP idea was hatched by Big Ten commissioner Tony Petitti and his staff in late summer 2025 after expansion talks to grow the bracket beyond the current 12-team format had fallen apart months earlier. His initial idea for 16 teams, with half of the berths reserved for Big Ten and SEC teams, was rejected by the other conferences, including the SEC. The 24-team concept was first met with dismissive skepticism as well, but opinions changed swiftly, and by spring of this year, leaders of the ACC, Big 12 and Notre Dame publicly supported the plan. The lone holdout was SEC commissioner Greg Sankey, but even as he cast doubt on such a drastic change and warned of potentially damaging the sport’s regular season, many of his constituents warmed to the idea of doubling the Playoff field. By September, when the CFP announced it was triggering an exclusive negotiating window with ESPN to work out a deal on expanding the Playoff and swapping in that inventory for conference championship games, the outcome was all but inevitable. It was fewer than 20 years ago that some college football leaders were so adamantly opposed to having a national playoff that the mere use of the word was frowned upon. The Bowl Championship Series, born in 1998, set up a No. 1 vs. No. 2 title game to end each season, and for many that was progress enough after decades of polls and a hodgepodge of bowls determining a champ . Advertisement The first real postseason tournament in major college football was a baby step. The four-team CFP was devised in 2012 and launched in 2014. Immediately, its impact was evident when Ohio State, a team that would have been on the outside of the BCS title game just a year earlier, made a late-season run to reach the Playoff as the fourth and final seed, then won it all. The problems with the four-team Playoff also quickly revealed themselves, as four spots meant at least one power conference would be left without any representation every year. That dragged down the reputations of conferences that missed out, and it also helped consolidate power among a small number of schools that kept getting into the four-team bracket. Alabama and Clemson met four straight years in the CFP, playing for the title in three of those years, and an undefeated UCF team was left out two years in a row. What You Should Read Next College Football Playoff expansion winners and losers, from fans to bowls to the Big Ten College football has changed a lot recently. Next season will being about another seismic shift: a 24-team Playoff. Who will be impacted? A plan to expand to 12 teams started brewing in 2019, was delayed by the COVID-19 pandemic in 2020, was unveiled in 2021 and, after some hard feelings among conference leaders about Texas and Oklahoma moving to the SEC, was finally implemented in 2024. By the time the 12-team Playoff debuted, it already felt misaligned with radically redrawn conference affiliations. The Pac-12’s demise had cut the Power 5 to a P4, and the Big Ten and SEC separated themselves from the ACC and Big 12 by adding traditional football powers such as Texas, Oklahoma, USC and Oregon and by insisting on a bigger cut of CFP revenue from the latest agreement among the conferences. By Year 2 of the 12-team Playoff, the conferences had removed the conference championship requirement for a bye. In Year 3 (this year), all four Power 4 conference champions are guaranteed a spot, and Notre Dame is guaranteed a spot for finishing ranked in the top 12. A move to a 24-team field in 2027 will mark five consecutive years with a change to the Playoff’s layout or format, but with this latest deal, talk of expansion is off the table for the near future. The contract calls for the CFP to be locked in at 24 teams for its duration through the 2031-32 season. Have questions about College Football Playoff expansion? Submit them here for our staff to answer . Nov 16, 2026 Connections: Sports Edition Spot the pattern. Connect the terms Find the hidden link between sports terms Play today's puzzle Tagged To: College Football Sports Business",
